# Amarchy: Super-key shortcuts
> Amarchy gives the whole machine keyboard shortcuts on the Left Amiga key, which it
> calls Super. It is started at boot and listed in Exchange as AMARCHY. Left Amiga +
> Space shows a quick menu you filter by typing; Left Amiga + K lists every key; Left
> Amiga + T opens a tab (or a window when no UP-Term window is open). The keys live in
> one text file you can read and edit.
$ cd "$start"
? Count the shipped key rows with grep -c '^super' ENVARC:up-term/keymap.
= case "$LEARN_LINE" in "grep -c '^super' ENVARC:up-term/keymap") true ;; *) false ;; esac
= [ "$(cat "$LEARN_OUT")" -gt 20 ]
~ Type grep -c '^super' ENVARC:up-term/keymap; the count is the number of rows.
! grep -c '^super' ENVARC:up-term/keymap
> A row reads  super+t = tab:new ; New tab | window : the key, the action, the label the
> viewer shows, and its category. Put your own rows in ENVARC:up-term/keymap.user (a row
> there replaces the row for the same key, and  super+t = none  unbinds one), then copy
> the file to ENV:up-term/keymap.user (Amarchy picks it up at once) or reboot.
? Ask Amarchy for its state with rx "address AMARCHY STATUS" (it answers with the row count).
= case "$LEARN_LINE" in "rx \"address AMARCHY STATUS\"") true ;; *) false ;; esac
= grep -q 'rows=' "$LEARN_OUT"
~ Type rx "address AMARCHY STATUS"; the answer holds rows= and enabled=.
! rx "address AMARCHY STATUS"
> Rows named win:<verb> (cycle, front, back, zip, fullwidth, fullscreen and more) act on
> windows of any program. There is no verb that closes another program's window.
> To switch it off for a while, open Exchange and Disable AMARCHY: every Left Amiga key
> then behaves as before. man amarchy has the whole file format.
> Directory Opus: when Directory Opus 5 is running, the DOpus group of the quick menu and
> Left Amiga + Shift + D (new lister), Left Amiga + Shift + O (lister at this folder) and
> Left Amiga + D (bring it to the front) drive it. Install it with  upkg install dopus .
> Without Directory Opus these keys do nothing.
